## Story Description
Three pure-addition files that provide the architectural foundation for all future analytics pages: (1) FilterContext.tsx moves filter state to React Context so it persists across page navigation; (2) aggregations.ts provides reusable metric calculations (ABC, concentration, tail spend, top-N) that pages import instead of reinventing; (3) useDrilldown.ts manages the overview→category→supplier→transaction navigation pattern with a DrilldownBreadcrumb component. The existing useFilters hook is refactored to delegate to FilterContext so existing pages need no changes.

## Acceptance Criteria
- Create file: frontend/src/contexts/FilterContext.tsx — exports: FilterContextValue interface {filters: FilterState, updateFilter: (key: keyof FilterState, value: any) => void, clearFilters: () => void, toQueryParams: () => Record<string,string>}; FilterProvider component (wraps children, uses useReducer with FilterState); useFilterContext() hook that throws if used outside provider
- FilterContext reducer handles actions: SET_FILTER (updates single key), CLEAR_ALL (resets to defaultFilters()). State is initialised from URL search params if present (reads ?date_from=&date_to=&legal_entities=& etc.) so filters survive page refresh.
- Edit file: frontend/src/hooks/useFilters.ts — replace useState with useFilterContext(). The hook's return signature {filters, updateFilter, clearFilters, toQueryParams} must remain IDENTICAL so all existing pages (OverviewPage, CategoryPage, SupplierPage, etc.) continue to work without changes.
- Edit file: frontend/src/main.tsx — wrap the App component in <FilterProvider> inside the existing QueryClientProvider wrapper
- Create file: frontend/src/lib/aggregations.ts — pure TypeScript functions (no React imports): calculateABCSegments(rows: {id: string, name: string, spend: number}[]): (row & {abc_segment: ABCSegment, cumulative_pct: number})[]; calculateSpendConcentration(rows: {spend: number}[], topN: number): {top_n_spend: number, top_n_pct: number, total_spend: number}; getTopN<T extends {spend?: number, total_spend?: number}>(rows: T[], n: number): T[]; calculateTailSpendMetrics(rows: {id: string, spend: number}[]): {tail_supplier_count: number, tail_spend: number, tail_pct: number, core_supplier_count: number}; formatSpendSummary(totalSpend: number, currency: string): {formatted: string, millions: number, billions: number, scale: 'K'|'M'|'B'}
- Create file: frontend/src/hooks/useDrilldown.ts — exports: type DrilldownLevel = 'overview' | 'category_l1' | 'category_l2' | 'supplier' | 'transaction'; interface DrilldownState {level: DrilldownLevel, category_l1?: string, category_l2?: string, supplier_id?: string, supplier_name?: string}; function useDrilldown(): {state: DrilldownState, drillTo: (level: DrilldownLevel, context: Partial<DrilldownState>) => void, drillUp: () => void, reset: () => void, isFiltered: boolean}. History stack allows drillUp() to navigate back.
- Create file: frontend/src/components/DrilldownBreadcrumb.tsx — renders nothing when state.level === 'overview'; renders a breadcrumb trail with clickable segments (Overview > {category_l1} > {category_l2} > {supplier_name}) based on DrilldownState; each segment calls drillUp() to the appropriate level. Uses shadcn/ui styling.
- Verify: cd frontend && npm run build — exits 0 with zero TypeScript errors after all new files and edits

